#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""wf-map — where to go in the code, GENERATED ON EVERY CALL AND NEVER STORED.

THE DEFECT, MEASURED. Four coder orders were profiled from their transcripts on
2026-10-12: **30-43% of their tool calls ran tests, 21-42% searched and read code, and
only 14-21% edited anything.** Two of them ran out of turns. The user, the same day:
*"if the bin check is taking a long time and the coder is running out of turns trying to
find data, it seems to me that both of these would be helped by some kind of an index
system to help direct where in the code to go for specific things."*

So this answers three questions an employee otherwise answers with a dozen reads:

    where in this file          `wf-map <file>`    sections and defs, with line numbers
    which files mention this    `wf-map --topic …`  ranked, bounded, pasteable
    what covers this file       `wf-map --tests …`  the checks, fixtures and prove cases

NOTHING IS STORED, AND THAT IS THE DESIGN RATHER THAN AN OMISSION. A hand-kept index
goes stale silently: the `lab` CLAUDE.md carried a roster of ~30 subprojects in which
**12 no longer existed on disk and 10 that did exist were missing**, and nothing
reported it for months. A map regenerated from the files cannot drift from them, and a
map on disk is one more shipped file to keep true. The cost of regenerating is one pass
over the text — MEASURED on this repository's own `bin/check` at 19,841 lines:
0.08 s. Under the one-second budget by an order of magnitude, so there is no cache to
justify.

IT NAMES THE SELECTORS THAT ALREADY EXIST AND NEVER KEEPS A SECOND LIST, and it does
not RUN them. `--tests` prints `bin/prove --cases-for <file>`, `bin/check --for <file>`
and `bin/script-conformance --list --touching <file>` for the caller to run
deliberately, beside what a text read can be sure of on its own. Those three decide
coverage for their own runs, so no copy of their answer is kept here — a second list of
"what covers what" is the stale index this script exists to avoid, one directory over.

WHAT IT REFUSES TO DO, because it reads whatever path it is handed:

  * **It never leaves the root.** Every target is resolved with `realpath` and must
    still be inside the resolved root, so a symlink pointing out of the tree is
    REFUSED by name rather than followed. The containment test happens before the open.
  * **It never executes ANYTHING it finds in the tree** — not the file it maps, and not
    a suite that happens to live there. Outlines come from reading text and matching
    regexes, never from `import` and never from a loader; coverage commands are
    PRINTED. The first version ran `bin/prove` out of the mapped root, gated on a
    docstring match, and a docstring is text anybody can copy: in a hostile checkout
    that gate would have run the checkout's code. There is no identity check an
    installed copy can make that tells a stranger's `bin/prove` from this project's, so
    the capability is gone rather than guarded.
  * **Every `git` call is hardened against repository-local configuration**, which can
    name an executable: `core.fsmonitor` and `core.hooksPath` are overridden on the
    command line, `GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0` keeps a read from writing, and both calls are
    argument lists with timeouts. A `git grep` in a repository somebody else wrote must
    not be a way to start their program.
  * **It caps what it reads** at CAP bytes per file, and says so when it truncates. A
    multi-gigabyte generated file must not become this process's memory footprint.
  * **No `shell=True`, ever.** The two tools it calls are invoked as argument lists,
    with a timeout, and only after their own first bytes identify them as this
    project's own (`SIGILS` below). A stranger's `bin/prove` is not run; that project
    takes the handbook path instead.

TWO BOUNDED DECISIONS, DECLARED SO NEITHER IS RE-LITIGATED:

    sec-eval: the containment test resolves the path and the open follows it, so a link
    swapped between the two would resolve differently. Bounded — this runs as the
    developer, inside a tree that developer already has, with no privilege boundary to
    cross, and an attacker who can swap a path in your checkout does not need this
    script. Upgrade to opening each component with O_NOFOLLOW if it ever runs as
    another user.

    sec-eval: a repo-relative path beginning with `-` is printed inside a suggested
    command rather than passed anywhere, so the caller reads it before any shell does.
    Upgrade to a `--` separator in the printed form if a path like that ever appears.

Exit codes
    0  mapped
    2  nothing could be read, or the target is outside the root

Usage
    wf-map <file> [<file>…]           outline: sections, defs, first doc line
    wf-map --topic <words…>           files and lines that match, ranked
    wf-map --tests <file>             what covers that file
    options  --root DIR  --limit N
"""

import argparse
import json
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys

CAP = 4 * 1024 * 1024          # bytes read per file; see § WHAT IT REFUSES TO DO
TOPIC_FILES = 12               # files reported per topic query
TOPIC_LINES = 3                # example lines per file
WIDTH = 108                    # a printed line is truncated to this, so output pastes
DEFAULT_LIMIT = 200            # outline rows before the deepest are dropped
TOOL_TIMEOUT = 120
WALK_BUDGET = 20000            # files the no-git fallback reads before it says it stopped

# EVERY VCS CALL IS HARDENED, because repository-local configuration can name an
# EXECUTABLE and this script runs in trees it did not write. `core.fsmonitor` and
# `core.hooksPath` are the two that start a program; both are overridden on the command
# line, where no in-repo config can win. `GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0` keeps a read-only query
# from writing to the index, and `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0` keeps it from ever asking for a
# credential. Same class the commitments hook is fixing (O7).
GIT = ["git", "-c", "core.fsmonitor=false", "-c", "core.hooksPath=/dev/null"]


def git_env():
    """The environment every vcs call gets: no optional writes, no prompts."""
    return dict(os.environ, GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS="0", GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT="0")

SKIP_DIRS = {".git", "node_modules", "__pycache__", ".venv", "venv", "dist", "build",
             "corpus", ".mypy_cache", ".pytest_cache", ".next", "target", "vendor"}

# A section banner on its own line: `# ── TITLE ──`, `## TITLE ##`, `/* == TITLE == */`.
BANNER_INLINE = re.compile(r"^\s*(?:#|//|--|;|/\*)+\s*[─━—=*#~_+-]{2,}\s*(\S.*?)\s*[─━—=*#~_+-]{2,}")
# A rule line whose TITLE is the comment line after it — `bin/check`'s own house style.
BANNER_RULE = re.compile(r"^\s*(?:#|//|--|;|/\*)+\s*[─━—=*#~_+-]{8,}\s*(?:\*/)?\s*$")
COMMENT_TEXT = re.compile(r"^\s*(?:#|//|--|;)+\s*(\S.*)$")

PY_DEF = re.compile(r"^(\s*)(?:async\s+)?(def|class)\s+([A-Za-z_]\w*)\s*(.*)$")
SH_DEF = re.compile(r"^(\s*)(?:function\s+)?([A-Za-z_][\w:.-]*)\s*\(\)\s*\{?")
MD_HEAD = re.compile(r"^(#{1,6})\s+(\S.*?)\s*#*$")
GENERIC_DEF = re.compile(
    r"^(\s*)(?:export\s+|public\s+|private\s+|protected\s+|static\s+|final\s+|async\s+)*"
    r"(def|class|function|func|fn|sub|type|interface|struct|impl|module|trait|package)\s+"
    r"([A-Za-z_$][\w$:.-]*)")
DOCSTRING = re.compile(r'^\s*(?:[rubf]{0,2})("""|\'\'\')\s*(.*?)\s*(?:"""|\'\'\')?\s*$')

# THE SUITES `--tests` NAMES, and it names them as COMMANDS FOR THE CALLER TO RUN. It
# does not run them. The first version did, gated on a docstring match that was supposed
# to prove the script was this project's own — and a docstring is text anybody can copy,
# so `wf-map --tests` inside a hostile checkout would have run that checkout's code
# (found in security review of fabecb3, before this reached anyone). No identity check
# available to an installed copy can tell a stranger's `bin/prove` from ours, so the
# class is removed rather than guarded: nothing in the mapped tree is ever executed.
COVERAGE_COMMANDS = (
    ("bin/prove", "bin/prove --cases-for {rel}",
     "the bin/check assertions whose case names this file"),
    ("bin/check", "bin/check --for {rel}",
     "run only those assertions"),
    ("bin/script-conformance", "bin/script-conformance --list --touching {rel}",
     "the conformance fixtures a change to this file can reach"),
)
EXPECTATIONS = "fixtures/scripts/expectations.json"


def clip(text, width=WIDTH):
    """One line, no tabs, bounded — every row printed goes through here."""
    one = " ".join(str(text).split())
    return one if len(one) <= width else one[: width - 1] + "…"


def inside(root, path):
    """The resolved path, or None when it escapes `root`.

    `realpath` resolves every link in the chain, so this is the one test that catches a
    symlink out of the tree. It is applied BEFORE any open, because following the link
    to find out where it goes is the thing being prevented.
    """
    rroot = os.path.realpath(root)
    real = os.path.realpath(path)
    if real == rroot or real.startswith(rroot + os.sep):
        return real
    return None


def read_capped(path):
    """`(text, truncated)` — or `(None, False)` when it cannot be read as text."""
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as fh:
            raw = fh.read(CAP + 1)
    except OSError:
        return None, False
    truncated = len(raw) > CAP
    raw = raw[:CAP]
    if b"\0" in raw[:8192]:
        return None, False          # binary: there is no outline to take
    return raw.decode("utf-8", errors="replace"), truncated


def language(path, text):
    """`python` | `shell` | `markdown` | `generic`, from the name then the shebang."""
    ext = os.path.splitext(path)[1].lower()
    if ext in (".py", ".pyi"):
        return "python"
    if ext in (".sh", ".bash", ".zsh", ".ksh"):
        return "shell"
    if ext in (".md", ".markdown", ".mdx"):
        return "markdown"
    head = text[:200]
    if head.startswith("#!"):
        first = head.splitlines()[0]
        if "python" in first:
            return "python"
        if any(s in first for s in ("sh", "bash", "zsh", "ksh")):
            return "shell"
    return "generic"


def first_doc(lines, i, lang):
    """The first docstring or comment line belonging to the definition on line `i`.

    Looks DOWN for a docstring (python) and UP for a comment block (every language),
    because those are where the one sentence that says what a thing is actually lives.
    """
    if lang == "python":
        for j in range(i + 1, min(i + 4, len(lines))):
            line = lines[j]
            if not line.strip():
                continue
            m = DOCSTRING.match(line)
            if m:
                if m.group(2):
                    return m.group(2)
                nxt = lines[j + 1] if j + 1 < len(lines) else ""
                return nxt.strip()
            break
    for j in range(i - 1, max(i - 4, -1), -1):
        line = lines[j]
        if not line.strip():
            break
        m = COMMENT_TEXT.match(line)
        if m and not BANNER_RULE.match(line):
            return m.group(1)
        break
    return ""


def banner(lines, i):
    """The section title declared at line `i`, or ""."""
    m = BANNER_INLINE.match(lines[i])
    if m and len(re.findall(r"\w", m.group(1))) >= 3:
        return m.group(1)
    if BANNER_RULE.match(lines[i]):
        for j in range(i + 1, min(i + 3, len(lines))):
            m2 = COMMENT_TEXT.match(lines[j])
            if m2 and not BANNER_RULE.match(lines[j]) and len(re.findall(r"\w", m2.group(1))) >= 3:
                return m2.group(1)
            if not lines[j].strip():
                break
    return ""


def outline(path, text, lang):
    """Rows of `(lineno, depth, kind, label, note)`, in file order.

    ONE PASS, and the regexes are the only work per line. That is what keeps a
    19,841-line file under a tenth of a second — an AST parse would be more precise on
    python and would refuse every file that does not currently parse, which is exactly
    the file somebody is mapping while they fix it.
    """
    lines = text.splitlines()
    rows = []
    for i, line in enumerate(lines):
        n = i + 1
        if lang == "markdown":
            m = MD_HEAD.match(line)
            if m:
                note = ""
                for j in range(i + 1, min(i + 8, len(lines))):
                    s = lines[j].strip()
                    # An HTML comment under a heading is an enforcement marker in this
                    # project's own references, never the sentence that says what the
                    # section is for.
                    if s and not s.startswith("<!--") and not MD_HEAD.match(lines[j]):
                        note = s
                        break
                rows.append((n, len(m.group(1)) - 1, "§", m.group(2), note))
            continue
        title = banner(lines, i)
        if title:
            rows.append((n, 0, "§", title, ""))
            continue
        m = PY_DEF.match(line) if lang == "python" else None
        if m:
            depth = len(m.group(1).expandtabs(4)) // 4
            rows.append((n, depth, m.group(2), m.group(3), first_doc(lines, i, lang)))
            continue
        if lang == "shell":
            m = SH_DEF.match(line)
            if m and m.group(2) not in ("if", "then", "else", "fi", "case", "esac", "while"):
                rows.append((n, 0, "fn", m.group(2), first_doc(lines, i, lang)))
            continue
        if lang == "generic":
            m = GENERIC_DEF.match(line)
            if m:
                depth = len(m.group(1).expandtabs(4)) // 4
                rows.append((n, depth, m.group(2), m.group(3), first_doc(lines, i, lang)))
                continue
            m = MD_HEAD.match(line)
            if m:
                rows.append((n, len(m.group(1)) - 1, "§", m.group(2), ""))
    return rows


def print_outline(rel, text, truncated, lang, limit):
    rows = outline(rel, text, lang)
    # OVER THE LIMIT, THE DEEPEST ROWS GO LAST. Truncating in file order would hand back
    # the top of a 19k-line file and call it a map of the file, so the budget is spent a
    # depth at a time: every section first, then every top-level def, then the nested
    # ones until it runs out. The limit is always FILLED — an early version dropped a
    # whole layer to stay under it and printed nothing at all for `bin/check`.
    kept = []
    for d in sorted({r[1] for r in rows}):
        layer = [r for r in rows if r[1] == d]
        if len(kept) + len(layer) <= limit:
            kept += layer
        else:
            kept += layer[: max(0, limit - len(kept))]
            break
    kept.sort(key=lambda r: r[0])
    dropped = len(rows) - len(kept)
    secs = sum(1 for r in rows if r[2] == "§")
    print(f"{rel}  ·  {lang} · {len(text.splitlines())} lines · "
          f"{len(rows) - secs} def(s) · {secs} section(s)"
          + ("  · TRUNCATED at %d bytes" % CAP if truncated else ""))
    for n, depth, kind, label, note in kept:
        # THE LINE NUMBER IS PRINTED OUTSIDE `clip`, which collapses runs of whitespace
        # and so eats both the column and the nesting indent when it is handed the
        # whole row. The number is the one column a reader scans.
        body = "  " * min(depth, 4) + f"{kind} {label}" + (f"    {note}" if note else "")
        print(f"{n:>6}  {clip(body, WIDTH - 8)}")
    if dropped:
        print(f"       … {dropped} deeper row(s) not shown — raise with --limit")
    print()


def git_root(root):
    """`root` when it is inside a git work tree and git is usable, else None."""
    try:
        # PYTHON 3.6 IS THE FLOOR — RHEL 8 ships it (DEF-Q-001) — so never
        # `capture_output=` or `text=`: both arrived in 3.7 and a 3.7 call crashes there.
        r = subprocess.run(GIT + ["-C", root, "rev-parse", "--is-inside-work-tree"],
                           stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE,
                           universal_newlines=True, timeout=30, env=git_env())
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None
    return root if r.returncode == 0 and r.stdout.strip() == "true" else None


def topic_git(root, words):
    """Per-word hits from `git grep`, which honours .gitignore for free.

    One invocation per word rather than one alternation, because the RANK needs to know
    how many of the words a file matched — a file carrying every word is the one worth
    opening first, and a single regex cannot report that.
    """
    hits = {}
    for w in words:
        try:
            r = subprocess.run(
                GIT + ["-C", root, "grep", "-n", "-I", "-i", "--untracked",
                       "--no-color", "-F", "-e", w],
                stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE,
                universal_newlines=True, timeout=TOOL_TIMEOUT, env=git_env())
        except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
            return None
        if r.returncode not in (0, 1):
            return None
        for line in r.stdout.splitlines():
            parts = line.split(":", 2)
            if len(parts) == 3 and parts[1].isdigit():
                hits.setdefault(parts[0], {"words": set(), "lines": {}})
                hits[parts[0]]["words"].add(w.lower())
                hits[parts[0]]["lines"][int(parts[1])] = parts[2]
    return hits


def topic_walk(root, words):
    """The no-git fallback: one bounded walk, text files only.

    TWO BOUNDS AND A SKIP, each because the git path gets the same protection for free
    and this one does not:

      * **dotfiles and dot-directories are skipped.** `git grep --untracked` never
        reports an ignored file, so a topic query in a repository cannot surface `.env`,
        a cached credential or a vendored tree. Without this, the SAME query in a
        directory that is not a repository would print those lines into an agent's
        context — the output of this tool is read by a model, which is a disclosure
        surface rather than a terminal somebody glances at.
      * **a file budget.** A walk over a home directory or a mounted volume would read
        for minutes; `git grep` is bounded by its own timeout. The budget is reported
        when it bites, because a truncated search that looks complete is worse than a
        slow one.
    """
    hits = {}
    low = [w.lower() for w in words]
    seen = 0
    for d, dirs, files in os.walk(root):
        dirs[:] = sorted(x for x in dirs if x not in SKIP_DIRS and not x.startswith("."))
        for f in sorted(files):
            if f.startswith("."):
                continue
            seen += 1
            if seen > WALK_BUDGET:
                print(f"… stopped after {WALK_BUDGET} files — narrow with --root, or "
                      f"run this inside a repository where git bounds the search")
                return hits
            p = os.path.join(d, f)
            if os.path.islink(p) or not inside(root, p):
                continue
            text, _ = read_capped(p)
            if text is None:
                continue
            rel = os.path.relpath(p, root)
            for i, line in enumerate(text.splitlines(), 1):
                ll = line.lower()
                for w in low:
                    if w in ll:
                        hits.setdefault(rel, {"words": set(), "lines": {}})
                        hits[rel]["words"].add(w)
                        hits[rel]["lines"][i] = line
    return hits


# A MATCHING LINE IS ONE HIT, however many of the query's words it carries, so the hits
# are keyed by line number. The first version appended one entry per word and printed the
# same line three times, which reads as three places to look.
def print_topic(root, words, limit):
    source = "git grep"
    hits = topic_git(root, words) if git_root(root) else None
    if hits is None:
        hits, source = topic_walk(root, words), "walk"
    ranked = sorted(hits.items(),
                    key=lambda kv: (-len(kv[1]["words"]), -len(kv[1]["lines"]), kv[0]))
    shown = ranked[: max(1, min(limit, TOPIC_FILES))]
    print(f"topic: {' '.join(words)}  ·  {len(ranked)} file(s) matched "
          f"({source}, {len(words)} word(s))")
    if not ranked:
        print("  nothing matched — try fewer or broader words\n")
        return 0
    for rel, info in shown:
        print(f"\n{rel}  ·  {len(info['lines'])} hit(s) · "
              f"{len(info['words'])}/{len(words)} word(s)")
        for n, line in sorted(info["lines"].items())[:TOPIC_LINES]:
            print(f"{n:>6}  {clip(line, WIDTH - 8)}")
    if len(ranked) > len(shown):
        print(f"\n… {len(ranked) - len(shown)} more file(s) — narrow the words")
    print()


def have(root, rel):
    """True when `rel` is a regular file inside `root` — read as a FACT, never run."""
    p = os.path.join(root, rel)
    return not os.path.islink(p) and os.path.isfile(p) and bool(inside(root, p))


def fixture_cases(root, rel):
    """Conformance cases naming `rel`, read STATICALLY out of `expectations.json`.

    JSON IS DATA; A SCRIPT IS NOT. `bin/script-conformance --list --touching` computes
    this more precisely — it follows one script loading another — but computing it means
    running a program out of the mapped tree, which is the thing this script does not
    do. So the rows here are the ones a text read can be sure of (a case whose `script`
    is this file, or whose own fixture directory is this path), and the command that
    computes the full set is PRINTED for the caller to run deliberately.
    """
    p = os.path.join(root, EXPECTATIONS)
    if not have(root, EXPECTATIONS):
        return []
    text, _ = read_capped(p)
    if not text:
        return []
    try:
        rows = json.loads(text)
    except ValueError:
        return []
    if not isinstance(rows, dict):
        return []
    base = os.path.basename(rel)
    out = []
    for name, spec in sorted(rows.items()):
        if not isinstance(spec, dict):
            continue
        if spec.get("script") == base or rel.startswith(f"fixtures/scripts/{name}/"):
            out.append(name)
    return out


def handbook_checks(root, rel):
    """Every handbook `Check:`/`Regression:`/`Negative:` line naming `rel` or its directory.

    THE PROJECT PATH, and it is the common one — a staffed project has no `bin/prove`.
    `handbook-templates.md` § Verification is the shape these lines come in, so the
    employees' own runnable proofs ARE the test map wherever this script has no suite to
    ask.
    """
    rx = re.compile(r"^\s*[-*]?\s*\**(Check|Regression|Negative)\**:\s*(\S.*)$")
    base = os.path.basename(rel)
    folder = os.path.dirname(rel)
    out = []
    agents = os.path.join(root, ".claude", "agents")
    for d, dirs, files in os.walk(agents):
        dirs[:] = sorted(x for x in dirs if x not in SKIP_DIRS)
        for f in sorted(files):
            if not f.endswith(".md"):
                continue
            p = os.path.join(d, f)
            if os.path.islink(p) or not inside(root, p):
                continue
            text, _ = read_capped(p)
            if not text:
                continue
            for i, line in enumerate(text.splitlines(), 1):
                m = rx.match(line)
                if not m:
                    continue
                if rel in line or base in line or (folder and folder in line):
                    out.append((os.path.relpath(p, root), i, m.group(1), m.group(2)))
    return out


def print_tests(root, rel, target):
    """What covers `rel`: the commands that answer it, the fixtures a text read is sure
    of, and the handbook checks that name it. NOTHING HERE IS EXECUTED."""
    print(f"tests covering {rel}\n")
    cmds = [(c.format(rel=rel), why) for tool, c, why in COVERAGE_COMMANDS
            if have(root, tool)]
    if cmds:
        print(f"  suites in this tree · {len(cmds)} command(s) — RUN THESE YOURSELF; "
              f"wf-map never executes a script it found in the tree")
        for c, why in cmds:
            print("    $ " + clip(f"{c}    # {why}", WIDTH - 8))
    cases = fixture_cases(root, rel)
    if cases:
        print(f"\n  conformance fixtures naming it · {len(cases)} "
              f"(read from {EXPECTATIONS}; the --touching command above computes the "
              f"transitive set)")
        for c in cases[:20]:
            print("    · " + clip(c, WIDTH - 8))
        if len(cases) > 20:
            print(f"    … {len(cases) - 20} more")
    rows = handbook_checks(root, rel)
    if rows or not cmds:
        print(f"\n  handbook checks · {len(rows)}")
        for hb, n, kind, cmd in rows[:20]:
            print("    · " + clip(f"{hb}:{n}  {kind}: {cmd}", WIDTH - 8))
        if len(rows) > 20:
            print(f"    … {len(rows) - 20} more")
        if not rows:
            print("    no handbook names this file in a Check:, Regression: or Negative: line")
    print()
    if target is None:
        print("note: the file is not on disk — reporting only what names it\n")


def main():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(add_help=False)
    ap.add_argument("paths", nargs="*")
    ap.add_argument("--topic", nargs="+", default=None)
    ap.add_argument("--tests", default=None)
    ap.add_argument("--root", default=None)
    ap.add_argument("--limit", type=int, default=DEFAULT_LIMIT)
    ap.add_argument("--help", "-h", action="store_true")
    args = ap.parse_args()
    if args.help or not (args.paths or args.topic or args.tests):
        print(__doc__[__doc__.index("Usage"):].rstrip())
        return 0 if args.help else 2

    root = os.path.abspath(args.root or os.environ.get("CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR") or os.getcwd())
    if not os.path.isdir(root):
        print(f"wf-map: root is not a directory: {root}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    if args.topic:
        print_topic(root, args.topic, args.limit)
        return 0

    if args.tests:
        rel = os.path.relpath(os.path.abspath(os.path.join(root, args.tests)), root)
        target = inside(root, os.path.join(root, args.tests))
        if rel.startswith(".."):
            print(f"wf-map: {args.tests} is outside the root {root}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        if target is None:
            # A path whose LINKS leave the tree is refused; a path that is simply
            # absent is not — "what covers a file I am about to write" is a fair
            # question, and nothing is read to answer it.
            if os.path.lexists(os.path.join(root, args.tests)):
                print(f"wf-map: {args.tests} resolves outside the root {root} — refusing",
                      file=sys.stderr)
                return 2
        print_tests(root, rel, target)
        return 0

    mapped = 0
    for p in args.paths:
        rel = os.path.relpath(os.path.abspath(os.path.join(root, p)), root)
        real = inside(root, os.path.join(root, p))
        if real is None or rel.startswith(".."):
            print(f"wf-map: {p} resolves outside the root {root} — refusing", file=sys.stderr)
            continue
        if os.path.isdir(real):
            print(f"wf-map: {rel} is a directory — map a file, or use --topic", file=sys.stderr)
            continue
        text, truncated = read_capped(real)
        if text is None:
            print(f"wf-map: {rel} is not readable as text", file=sys.stderr)
            continue
        print_outline(rel, text, truncated, language(rel, text), args.limit)
        mapped += 1
    # NOTHING MAPPED EXITS NONZERO. An empty map and a map of an empty file read the
    # same on stdout, and the caller is usually a script.
    return 0 if mapped else 2


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
